159	164	166	167	the man	48	" Good night t'ee , "
172	179	181	182	the parson	49	" Good night , Sir John , "
199	246	253	254	the parson	49	" Now , sir , begging your pardon ; we met last market - day on this road about this time , and I said ' Good night , ' and you made reply ' _ Good night , Sir John _ , ' as now . "
247	251	253	254	the parson	49	" I did , "
256	267	253	254	the parson	49	" And once before that -- near a month ago . "
268	273	253	254	the parson	49	" I may have . "
274	302	253	254	the parson	49	" Then what might your meaning be in calling me ' Sir John ' these different times , when I be plain Jack Durbeyfield , the haggler ? "
312	319	320	320	he	49	" It was only my whim , "
331	422	320	320	he	49	" It was on account of a discovery I made some little time ago , whilst I was hunting up pedigrees for the new county history . I am Parson Tringham , the antiquary , of Stagfoot Lane . Do n't you really know , Durbeyfield , that you are the lineal representative of the ancient and knightly family of the d'Urbervilles , who derive their descent from Sir Pagan d'Urberville , that renowned knight who came from Normandy with William the Conqueror , as appears by Battle Abbey Roll ? "
423	431	253	254	the parson	49	" Never heard it before , sir ! "
432	643	320	320	he	49	" Well it 's true . Throw up your chin a moment , so that I may catch the profile of your face better . Yes , that 's the d'Urberville nose and chin -- a little debased . Your ancestor was one of the twelve knights who assisted the Lord of Estremavilla in Normandy in his conquest of Glamorganshire . Branches of your family held manors over all this part of England ; their names appear in the Pipe Rolls in the time of King Stephen . In the reign of King John one of them was rich enough to give a manor to the Knights Hospitallers ; and in Edward the Second 's time your forefather Brian was summoned to Westminster to attend the great Council there . You declined a little in Oliver Cromwell 's time , but to no serious extent , and in Charles the Second 's reign you were made Knights of the Royal Oak for your loyalty . Aye , there have been generations of Sir Johns among you , and if knighthood were hereditary , like a baronetcy , as it practically was in old times , when men were knighted from father to son , you would be Sir John now . "
644	651	253	254	the parson	49	" Ye do n't say so ! "
652	656	658	659	the parson	49	" In short , "
669	679	658	659	the parson	49	" there 's hardly such another family in England . "
680	690	692	692	Durbeyfield	21	" Daze my eyes , and is n't there ? "
694	740	692	692	Durbeyfield	21	" And here have I been knocking about , year after year , from pillar to post , as if I was no more than the commonest feller in the parish ... And how long hev this news about me been knowed , Pa'son Tringham ? "
830	847	849	849	he	54	" At first I resolved not to disturb you with such a useless piece of information , "
851	877	849	849	he	54	" However , our impulses are too strong for our judgement sometimes . I thought you might perhaps know something of it all the while . "
878	1027	801	801	Durbeyfield	21	" Well , I have heard once or twice , ' tis true , that my family had seen better days afore they came to Blackmoor . But I took no notice o't , thinking it to mean that we had once kept two horses where we now keep only one . I 've got a wold silver spoon , and a wold graven seal at home , too ; but , Lord , what 's a spoon and seal ? ... And to think that I and these noble d'Urbervilles were one flesh all the time . ' Twas said that my gr't - granfer had secrets , and did n't care to talk of where he came from ... And where do we raise our smoke , now , parson , if I may make so bold ; I mean , where do we d'Urbervilles live ? "
1028	1044	849	849	he	54	" You do n't live anywhere . You are extinct -- as a county family . "
1045	1050	801	801	Durbeyfield	21	" That 's bad . "
1051	1075	849	849	he	54	" Yes -- what the mendacious family chronicles call extinct in the male line -- that is , gone down -- gone under . "
1076	1083	801	801	Durbeyfield	21	" Then where do we lie ? "
1084	1110	849	849	he	54	" At Kingsbere - sub - Greenhill : rows and rows of you in your vaults , with your effigies under Purbeck - marble canopies . "
1111	1121	801	801	Durbeyfield	21	" And where be our family mansions and estates ? "
1122	1128	849	849	he	54	" You have n't any . "
1129	1136	801	801	Durbeyfield	21	" Oh ? No lands neither ? "
1137	1192	849	849	he	54	" None ; though you once had 'em in abundance , as I said , for you family consisted of numerous branches . In this county there was a seat of yours at Kingsbere , and another at Sherton , and another in Millpond , and another at Lullstead , and another at Wellbridge . "
1193	1204	801	801	Durbeyfield	21	" And shall we ever come into our own again ? "
1205	1214	849	849	he	54	" Ah -- that I ca n't tell ! "
1215	1227	1229	1229	Durbeyfield	21	" And what had I better do about it , sir ? "
1235	1292	849	849	he	54	" Oh -- nothing , nothing ; except chasten yourself with the thought of ' how are the mighty fallen . ' It is a fact of some interest to the local historian and genealogist , nothing more . There are several families among the cottagers of this county of almost equal lustre . Good night . "
1293	1343	1392	1392	Durbeyfield	21	" But you 'll turn back and have a quart of beer wi ' me on the strength o't , Pa'son Tringham ? There 's a very pretty brew in tap at The Pure Drop -- though , to be sure , not so good as at Rolliver 's . "
1344	1362	849	849	he	54	" No , thank you -- not this evening , Durbeyfield . You 've had enough already . "
1467	1487	1458	1459	the lad	62	" Boy , take up that basket ! I want ' ee to go on an errand for me . "
1495	1527	1458	1459	the lad	62	" Who be you , then , John Durbeyfield , to order me about and call me ' boy ' ? You know my name as well as I know yours ! "
1528	1600	1608	1608	Durbeyfield	37	" Do you , do you ? That 's the secret -- that 's the secret ! Now obey my orders , and take the message I 'm going to charge ' ee wi ' ... Well , Fred , I do n't mind telling you that the secret is that I 'm one of a noble race -- it has been just found out by me this present afternoon , P.M. "
1642	1653	1655	1657	the prostrate man	65	" Sir John d'Urberville -- that 's who I am , "
1659	1696	1655	1657	the prostrate man	65	" That is if knights were baronets -- which they be . ' Tis recorded in history all about me . Dost know of such a place , lad , as Kingsbere - sub - Greenhill ? "
1697	1708	1627	1628	The lad	62	" Ees . I 've been there to Greenhill Fair . "
1709	1720	1655	1657	the prostrate man	65	" Well , under the church of that city there lie-- "
1721	1752	1627	1628	The lad	62	" ' Tisn't a city , the place I mean ; leastwise ' twaddn ' when I was there--'twas a little one - eyed , blinking sort o ' place . "
1753	1826	1655	1657	the prostrate man	65	" Never you mind the place , boy , that 's not the question before us . Under the church of that there parish lie my ancestors -- hundreds of 'em -- in coats of mail and jewels , in gr't lead coffins weighing tons and tons . There 's not a man in the county o ' South - Wessex that 's got grander and nobler skillentons in his family than I. "
1827	1830	1627	1628	The lad	62	" Oh ? "
1831	1949	1655	1657	the prostrate man	65	" Now take up that basket , and goo on to Marlott , and when you 've come to The Pure Drop Inn , tell 'em to send a horse and carriage to me immed'ately , to carry me hwome . And in the bottom o ' the carriage they be to put a noggin o ' rum in a small bottle , and chalk it up to my account . And when you 've done that goo on to my house with the basket , and tell my wife to put away that washing , because she need n't finish it , and wait till I come hwome , as I 've news to tell her . "
